---
import PointDefenceTurning from "../../../components/studies/PointDefenceTurning.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "ion-point-defence-2026-11-08",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A small turret that turns to meet the threat</h2>
    <p>
      Ion Point Defence becomes the game’s baseline defence. Most equipment
      needs a schematic bought with credits; as starter equipment, this one
      never has to be bought, and every other defence is measured against it. It
      sits in a weapon slot, turns towards the closest target and fires a light
      ion shot. The shot belongs to the Light Ion Cannon’s family, a thin blue
      instant ray, with half its reach and, as a starting assumption, half its
      damage. Like the cannon it fires automatically, without a button.
    </p>
    <p>
      The central tension is legibility against restraint. The player should see
      the turret working: its round base turning as one solid object that
      carries the barrel and housing with it, a moment of alignment, then the
      shot. Yet a baseline fitting is always present on a small hull, so it must
      not compete with flight or with the ship’s character. Whether a base of a
      few pixels still reads as rotating at flight scale, and what turning speed
      feels deliberate rather than snapping or sluggish, are open questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      This cycle will settle the turret’s form, how its rotation reads, its shot
      and its baseline behaviour: reach, firing rhythm, turning speed and which
      target counts as closest. The supplied concept with the round base is the
      likely form; the version without one is a comparison. Combat balance,
      energy cost and the future of the existing red laser turret remain
      adjacent questions.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/ion-point-defence-2026-11-08/concept.png"
        alt="Supplied concept: the Light Ion Cannon for scale, a point-defence turret with a dark round base under its barrel and pale housing, and the same turret without the base"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Supplied concept. Left, a ship with fitted weapons for comparison;
        centre, the turret on its round base; right, the turret without a base.
        The turret uses the Light Ion Cannon’s parts, with a shorter barrel,
        channel and side housing. The
        <a href="/studies/ion-point-defence-2026-11-08/concept.svg"
          >original SVG</a
        >
        also fits both weapons to Scout, Wasp and Fighter at a common scale.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <details id="turning">
    <summary>02.1 / What makes the base read as turning? — B selected</summary>
    <h2>02.1 / What makes the base read as turning?</h2>
    <p>
      A plain disc looks the same at every angle, so it cannot show rotation by
      itself. A keeps the supplied base, lit from the ship’s left and therefore
      still while the barrel and housing turn over it. B keeps that fixed
      lighting but gives the base six rim slots and one pale index mark that
      turn with the turret, so the disc itself is seen to move. C removes the
      base; the cannon parts turn around the crossbar alone. The hypothesis is
      that B carries the turning on the base without breaking the ship’s light.
    </p>
    <PointDefenceTurning client:load />
    <p>
      Each turret sits on the ship’s first free hull weapon mount, drawn by the
      production ship renderer with the current starter outfit. For size, the
      production Light Ion Cannon takes the next weapon-capable mount, replacing
      a starter part where no mount is free; Scout has only one such mount and
      shows the turret alone. A target stand-in jumps to a new bearing every 1.6
      seconds, 55 units out, and drifts slowly; all three turrets turn at the
      same provisional 180°/s and share one deterministic path, without
      randomness. The close-up marks only the target’s bearing; the builder view
      shows the whole ship, and the flight view shows true size and range at one
      CSS pixel per unit. The 6- and 10-unit bases remain available for
      comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      This isolates form and the reading of rotation. There is no shot,
      targeting rule, damage, flight or camera motion; turning speed is fixed
      for the comparison and remains open. Fighter and Corvette carry the turret
      on top of the hull, where it can turn freely. Scout, Wasp and Fiddler draw
      these weapon mounts beneath the hull: the Scout hides the turret
      completely and the Wasp half of it. A starter turret has to work on every
      ship, so where it sits on these hulls is a fitting question for a later
      round, not a property of the three forms.
    </p>
    <p>
      B / Base carries turning marks is selected, with an 8-unit base diameter
      judged beside the 16-unit Light Ion Cannon on the same ships. Its rim
      slots and index mark turn with the turret while the base keeps the ship’s
      light. Turning speed remains open; later rounds use B at this size.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details id="speed">
    <summary>02.2 / How fast should it turn? — 180°/s selected</summary>
    <h2>02.2 / How fast should it turn?</h2>
    <p>
      The selected B turret now turns at 90, 180 and 360°/s. The fastest matches
      the existing red laser turret. The hypothesis is that a deliberate turn
      shows the turret working: the player sees it swing, settle and point,
      while a turn so fast that it snaps hides the motion that 02.1 made
      readable, and a slow one leaves the turret visibly behind the threat.
    </p>
    <PointDefenceTurning
      mode="speed"
      label="The selected point-defence turret tracking a scripted target at three turning speeds, enlarged and at flight scale"
      client:load
    />
    <p>
      Every column uses the same ship, mount, cannon fitting, 8-unit B turret
      and target script as 02.1. Turning the ship adds the production base turn
      rate of 2.25 rad/s, about 129°/s, in a fixed left–right sequence with no
      net rotation per loop. The target keeps its place in the world, so the
      turret has to cancel the ship’s turn as well as follow the target. At
      90°/s a ship turning away can outrun the turret.
    </p>
    <p>
      Speed is judged here without a shot. How long the turret must stay aligned
      before firing, and whether that delay changes the preferred speed, belongs
      with the shot itself. Ship-specific turn multipliers and the Scout, Wasp
      and Fiddler fitting remain outside this comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      180°/s is selected. It reads as slightly slow, while 360°/s is clearly too
      fast: the turret snaps rather than swings. Later rounds keep 180°/s.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details id="shot">
    <summary>
      02.3 / Alignment, then a shorter ion shot — 3 shots/s selected
    </summary>
    <h2>02.3 / Alignment, then a shorter ion shot</h2>
    <p>
      The selected turret now fires. Each shot is the Light Ion Cannon’s instant
      blue ray with half its reach: the production light profile, transparent
      tip and 65% dispersal, compressed to 100 units and stopped at the target.
      A turret fires only once its barrel is within 2° of the target and the
      target is in reach. The columns compare 1.5, 3 and 6 shots per second; the
      cannon fires three.
    </p>
    <PointDefenceTurning
      mode="shot"
      label="The selected point-defence turret aligning and firing a 100-unit ion ray at three firing rates, close-up and at flight scale"
      client:load
    />
    <p>
      The hypothesis is that the half-length ray still reads as the cannon’s
      family, and that the gap between turning and the first shot makes the
      turret’s work visible. The target now changes range as well as bearing;
      the third position, 120 units out, lies beyond reach, so no turret fires
      at it. The dashed ring marks the 100-unit reach around the mount.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a fixture, not production simulation. The flash follows the
      current muzzle for 80 ms and the dispersal stays where it was fired, as in
      the cannon, but decays by its 0.1-second half-life without the shared
      effects surface. There is no damage, energy, impact plume or occlusion by
      the ship’s own hull. Damage per shot and energy cost follow the firing
      rhythm rather than determine it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three shots per second is selected, the same rhythm as the Light Ion
      Cannon. The 100-unit ray, 2° alignment and 180°/s turning carry forward.
      Damage per shot remains the assumed half of the cannon’s.
    </p>
  </details>
  <section id="fitting">
    <h2>02.4 / A turret beneath the hull — accepted as hidden</h2>
    <p>
      As starter equipment the turret must work on every ship, but Scout, Wasp
      and Fiddler draw all their weapon mounts beneath the hull. Production
      mount layering is kept: the turret remains a fitting under the hull on
      these ships, and choosing another mount cannot help, because none of their
      weapon mounts is above it. Under that layering the Scout hides the turret
      entirely, the Wasp half of it and the Fiddler’s blades part of it; only
      the protruding barrel and the ray show its work.
    </p>
    <PointDefenceTurning
      mode="fitting"
      label="The selected point-defence turret under production mount layering, firing at three shots per second"
      client:load
    />
    <p>
      The example shows the selected turret: B at 8 units, 180°/s, 3 shots per
      second and a 100-unit ray, on the same mount and target script as 02.3,
      under production layering. It starts on Scout; select Wasp or Fiddler to
      compare the partly hidden cases and Fighter or Corvette for a turret on
      top of the hull.
    </p>
    <p>
      A hidden turret is accepted. Where a ship carries its weapon mounts
      beneath the hull, the point defence is read through its protruding barrel
      and its ray; no marker is added to the hull. Its turning is therefore
      fully visible only on ships whose mounts sit above the hull.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="readiness">
    <h2>03 / Ready to scope a starter turret</h2>
    <p>
      The accepted design is a rotating ion turret on any weapon-capable hull
      mount. Its form is B, drawn from the supplied concept: the Light Ion
      Cannon’s parts, with a shorter barrel, channel and side housing, on a
      round base with an 8-unit diameter, whose six rim slots and pale index
      mark turn while its light stays with the ship. It turns at 180°/s, fires
      only within 2° of its target, three times a second, and casts the cannon’s
      instant blue ray over 100 units: the same light profile and transparent
      tip compressed to that reach, stopped at first contact. Production mount
      layering is kept, so on Scout, Wasp and Fiddler the turret is partly or
      wholly hidden and reads through its barrel and ray.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most of the behaviour exists. The laser already gives each mount its own
      turning angle, finite turn rate, alignment tolerance, retained target,
      cooldown and power debit, and its angle already reaches the builder
      renderer, so a hidden or visible turret can be drawn turning without new
      plumbing. The cannon supplies the instant ray: first physical contact with
      star and planet blocking, the 80 ms light-effects flash that follows the
      muzzle, 65% dispersal on the persistent effects surface and the impact
      plume. The validated part catalogue, saved fittings and Hangar handoff
      accept a new weapon part as they accepted the cannon.
    </p>
    <p>
      Delivery needs adaptations rather than new systems. The turret needs a
      genuine part identifier, Zod definition and shared procedural artwork with
      its muzzle derived from the same geometry. The study traced the concept
      image; production artwork should convert the supplied SVG, whose turret
      keeps the cannon’s barrel width, colours and crossbar but shortens the
      barrel from 19.5 to 16.9 source units, the channel from 10.4 to 8.8 and
      the side housing from 9.0 to 7.9, on two concentric base discs. Against
      the cannon’s accepted 16-unit flight length, its base measures about 8.9
      units across; the selected 8-unit diameter scales that geometry, and the
      trace agrees with the SVG within its proportions. The cached ion light
      profile is fixed at 200 units and must accept the turret’s 100-unit reach
      without stretching or changing the cannon. Targeting must follow the
      closest target, which replaces the laser’s threat priority for this part
      rather than changing the laser.
    </p>
    <p>
      Four choices remain open, each bounded. Damage is assumed at half the
      cannon’s, which would be 8, 6, 4 and 2 in four 25-unit bands; energy per
      shot and whether the shared ten-power reserve applies are not set, and
      both are balance values to confirm before implementation. The fixture kept
      turning towards a target beyond reach without firing; whether production
      tracks only targets within 100 units, or pre-aims at the closest one
      beyond it, is an integration choice. Closest needs a defined measure:
      distance from the turret to actual collision geometry, with collectible
      energy excluded as for other weapons.
    </p>
    <p>
      Starter status cannot be delivered yet. No economy, schematic or ownership
      system exists; the Hangar specification reserves those rules for their own
      contracts and the 0.6.0 plan excludes them. The intent that this turret
      never has to be bought is recorded for that future contract. Fitting it
      into the fixed starter outfits is a separate decision, since existing
      starter fittings are specified as unchanged. Whether the red laser remains
      alongside it is likewise adjacent. None of these blocks a playable alpha
      turret offered through the Hangar.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="scope">
    <h2>04 / Deliver one playable starter turret</h2>
    <p>
      The implementation target is a playable alpha Ion Point Defence, fitted
      through the existing Hangar and exercised in a real encounter. Retain form
      B with an 8-unit base diameter, existing weapon-capable hull mounts and
      production layering, 180°/s turning, 2° alignment, three shots per second,
      a 100-unit instant ray with the cannon’s light profile compressed to that
      reach, first-target stopping, the impact plume, the 80 ms flash and 65%
      dispersal. Damage, energy and tracking range stay provisional until
      confirmed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Design-principle disclosure:</strong>
      no change to
      <code>docs/design.md</code>
      is proposed. The turret fires automatically, adds no button and does not
      retune movement. Production implementation is separate work.
    </p>

    <h3>1 / Equipment and shared geometry — medium</h3>
    <p>
      Add a genuine Ion Point Defence part identifier and a validated weapon
      definition before changing consumers: reach, turning speed, alignment
      tolerance, cooldown, damage and energy defined together. Provisional
      values are half the cannon’s damage, 8, 6, 4 and 2 in 25-unit bands, and
      an energy cost per shot still to be set. Draw the turret as shared
      procedural Canvas artwork converted from the supplied SVG at the selected
      8-unit base diameter, adding B’s rim slots and index mark. Derive the
      muzzle from that geometry; turning uses the weapon angle the renderer
      already receives, so hidden mounts stay hidden.
    </p>
    <p>
      Owners: mode-a loadout and salvage Zod schemas, part catalogue, build
      renderer and a shared drawing owner beside the cannon artwork. Add a
      turret contract under
      <code>docs/specs/game/</code>
      alongside the implementation. Check compatible and incompatible slots,
      geometry and muzzle agreement, rotation in Preview and Plan, existing
      saved-loadout parsing and unchanged existing equipment. No new dependency
      is needed.
    </p>

    <h3>2 / Closest-target turning and instant shots — large</h3>
    <p>
      Depends on slice 1. Give the turret its own weapon state in the
      simulation, reusing the laser’s per-mount angle, finite turn rate,
      tolerance, target retention and cooldown, but choosing the closest
      damageable target by distance to its actual collision geometry, with
      collectible energy excluded and star and planets blocking the line. Fire
      when aligned within 2° and the first contact lies within 100 units;
      resolve damage once by muzzle-to-contact distance and start a one-third
      second cooldown. Generalise the cannon’s instant contact to take the
      part’s reach rather than duplicating it. The laser keeps its threat
      priority and the cannon its forward trigger.
    </p>
    <p>
      Confirm before implementing: the damage values, energy per shot, whether
      the shared ten-power reserve applies, and whether the turret tracks only
      targets within reach or pre-aims at the closest target beyond it. The
      study fixture pre-aimed; either answer is a small change. Evidence:
      deterministic tests for closest-target choice and its ties, turning
      limits, alignment gating, band boundaries, first-contact stopping, star
      and planet blocking, cooldown, power handling and pause or reset.
    </p>

    <h3>3 / Shorter ion light and impact — medium</h3>
    <p>
      Depends on slices 1 and 2. Let the cached ion light profile take a reach,
      keeping the cannon’s 200-unit profile unchanged and adding the turret’s
      100-unit profile with its own transparent tip. The flash follows the
      turret’s current muzzle for 80 ms; only dispersal stamps into the
      persistent effects surface at 65%; the existing impact plume marks the
      contact. A flash from a hidden turret leaves its muzzle under the hull, as
      accepted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Owners: salvage renderer, FlightFx, FlightDeck and the mixed-effects
      bench; update
      <code>docs/specs/factory/effects/spec.md</code>
      for the second profile. Check muzzle attachment while the turret and ship
      turn, dispersal staying in world space, pause, reset, wrap, camera pan and
      zoom, and several turrets with cannons and engines against the existing
      effects budget. Passing tests do not establish visual fidelity.
    </p>

    <h3>4 / Hangar delivery and playable review — medium</h3>
    <p>
      Catalogue work can follow slice 1; end-to-end review depends on slices 2
      and 3. Offer the turret through existing weapon-capable slots with its
      reach, rhythm, damage and energy in the builder text. Keep starter
      outfits, the red laser and keyboard and landscape touch controls
      unchanged. Owners: ShipBuilder and its catalogue, loadout storage and
      handoff; update
      <code>docs/specs/game/hangar/spec.md</code>
      and the turret contract with delivered behaviour.
    </p>
    <p>
      Finish with a playable encounter in the equipment Lab and this study,
      beside the Light Ion Cannon’s, showing closest-target switching, a target
      out of reach, a hit stopped at first contact, a turning ship and a hidden
      turret on Scout. Run the affected unit, browser, type, contract and build
      gates; report failures and unavailable checks separately. Human review
      judges turning, rhythm and balance independently.
    </p>

    <h3>Delivery boundary</h3>
    <p>
      These are four reviewable slices, not independent releases. Geometry
      enables simulation and catalogue work; simulation supplies the shot events
      that light and impact present; the encounter joins them. The largest
      uncertainty is balance: damage, energy and reserve policy. Starter status
      waits for the future economy contract; fitting the turret into the fixed
      starter outfits, retiring or keeping the red laser, mount redesign or
      layering changes, gravity or friction changes, paid services and
      publication are excluded. Production behaviour remains unchanged by this
      scope document.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
section > p {
  max-width: 72ch;
}
figure {
  margin: var(--spacing-lg) 0;
}
img {
  display: block;
  width: auto;
  max-width: 100%;
  height: 420px;
  object-fit: contain;
  background: var(--color-void);
}
figcaption {
  margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
  max-width: 72ch;
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
</style>
